  "slug": "lifespan-elegans-2c9e",
  "summary": "Long-read avian genomes, Xiphophorus pigmentation and acorn worm single-cell data test how regulatory and coding changes shape trait diversity.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is how genetic and regulatory changes across lineages and human populations map onto trait variation. In Xiphophorus evelynae, the confetti caudal tail-spotting phenotype shares an xmrk-linked genetic basis with tailspots in other species, indicating convergent molecular evolution of a cancer-associated pigmentation trait. Comparative analysis of long-read avian genomes links independent origins of behavioral innovation to positive selection and constrained regulatory evolution. In Saccoglossus kowalevskii, single-cell RNA-seq shows that neuronal cell types diverge widely across animal phyla around a shared core of neuronal genes. A human preprint intersects ancestral and hominin-derived variants with ClinVar, gnomAD and GTEx, reporting cerebellum-linked expression differences. What is new is the joint use of lineage-level comparative data and population or clinical resources to ask which evolutionary changes in regulation and coding underlie phenotypic diversity.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
